    "body": "<p>Bats that hunt in darkness navigate and find prey by echolocation: they emit ultrasonic pulses, mostly above the range of human hearing, and listen to the returning echoes to build a picture of their surroundings. Pulse design varies by hunting style. Open-air hunters use long, narrowband calls that carry far; clutter specialists use short, broadband frequency-modulated sweeps that resolve fine detail among leaves; and some bats adjust pulse rate as they close on prey, ending in a rapid feeding buzz of many pulses per second. Echo delay tells distance, echo strength and Doppler shift tell size and movement, and the two ears together tell direction. A bat can therefore intercept a moth in total darkness with a precision no human sense matches unaided. Echolocation calls are biophony of a special kind: they are communication with the environment itself, not with another animal.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: echolocation was demonstrated experimentally in the 20th century when researchers showed that bats with blocked ears, but not bats with blocked mouths or eyes, failed to avoid obstacles, and later work recorded the actual ultrasonic pulses with specialised microphones. Neurophysiological studies mapped auditory neurons tuned to echo delay, confirming that bat brains compute target distance from the time between pulse and echo. Species identification from recorded calls is now a standard survey method.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: echo distance equals speed of sound times delay divided by two, because the sound travels out and back. For a delay of 0.006 seconds, distance = 343 x 0.006 / 2 = 1.029 metres, about 1.03 metres. The demo prints 1.03 m for 6 milliseconds, 2.06 m for 12 milliseconds, and 0.51 m for 3 milliseconds.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published bat echolocation literature (Griffin and Galambos foundational experiments; modern call-identification surveys); speed of sound 343 m/s 